Marker: INTERVIEW-12-dbb399f495f975d2.

Use interval dynamic programming, choosing each balloon as the last burst between two surviving boundaries. Compute shorter intervals first so both subproblems are already optimal. Time: O(n³); space: O(n²).

```javascript
function solve(nums) {
  const values = [1, ...nums, 1];
  const m = values.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(m).fill(0));

  for (let width = 2; width < m; width++) {
    for (let left = 0; left + width < m; left++) {
      const right = left + width;
      let best = -Infinity;

      for (let k = left + 1; k < right; k++) {
        const coins = dp[left][k] + dp[k][right]
          + values[left] * values[k] * values[right];
        best = Math.max(best, coins);
      }

      dp[left][right] = best;
    }
  }

  return dp[0][m - 1];
}
```

Call solve(nums) directly; no input/output setup is needed. It returns 167 for [3, 1, 5, 8] and 0 for an empty array, without modifying nums.

