Use coordinate compression and a Fenwick tree, scanning right to left. Querying ranks below the current rank counts strictly smaller values, excluding duplicates. Time: O(n log n); space: O(n). Call solve(nums) directly; it returns the counts without modifying nums or performing input/output.

```javascript
function solve(nums) {
  const values = [...new Set(nums)].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const ranks = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
    ranks.set(values[i], i + 1);
  }

  const tree = new Array(values.length + 1).fill(0);
  const result = new Array(nums.length);

  for (let i = nums.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    const rank = ranks.get(nums[i]);
    let count = 0;

    for (let j = rank - 1; j > 0; j -= j & -j) {
      count += tree[j];
    }
    result[i] = count;

    for (let j = rank; j < tree.length; j += j & -j) {
      tree[j]++;
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

